WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.490
When we observe
the physical world,

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.885
we find a random
fluctuations everywhere.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.690
We can generate
truly random numbers

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:28.930
by measuring random
fluctuations known as noise.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.990
When we measure this
noise, known a sampling,

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.250
we can obtain numbers.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.230
For example, if we measure
the electric current

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:42.177
of TV static over time, we
will generate a truly random

00:00:42.177 --> 00:00:42.677
sequence.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:47.950
We can visualize
this random sequence

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.380
by drawing a path
that changes direction

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.080
according to each number,
known as a random walk.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.850
Notice the lack of
pattern at all scales.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.315
At each point in the
sequence, the next move

00:01:00.315 --> 00:01:01.450
is always unpredictable.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:07.760
Random processes are said
to be non-deterministic,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.860
since they're impossible
to determine in advance.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.620
Machines on the other
hand, are deterministic.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.480
Their operation
predictable and repeatable.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.220
In 1946, John von
Neumann was involved

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:24.910
in running computations for the
military, specifically involved

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:28.910
in the design of
the hydrogen bomb.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.540
Using a computer
called the ENIAC,

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.690
he planned to
repeatedly calculate

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:37.050
approximations of the processes
involved in nuclear fission.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.720
However, this required
quick access to randomly

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.580
generated numbers that
could be repeated if needed.

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.400
However, the ENIAC had very
limited internal memory.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.450
Storing long random
sequences was not possible.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.550
So Neumann developed
an algorithm

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:56.150
to mechanically simulate the
scrambling aspect of randomness

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.200
as follows.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.130
First, select a truly random
number, called the seed.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.760
This number comes from
the measurement of noise,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.360
or the current time
in milliseconds.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.950
Next, this seed is provided as
input to a simple calculation.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.480
Multiply this seed by itself.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.400
And then output the
middle of this result.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.130
Then you use this
output as the next seed

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:24.180
and repeat the process
as many times as needed.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.830
This is known as the
middle-squares method,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.290
and is just the first in a long
line of pseudo random number

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:33.910
generators.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.470
The randomness of
the sequence is

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.980
dependent on the randomness
of the initial seed only.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.200
Same seed, same sequence.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.580
So what are the differences
between a randomly generated

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.660
versus pseudorandomly
generated sequence?

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.080
Let's represent each
sequence as a random walk.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:03.150
They seem similar until
we speed things up.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.970
The pseudorandom sequence
must eventually repeat.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:08.910
This occurs when the
algorithm reaches

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:13.180
a seed it has previously
used, and the cycle repeats.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.480
The lengths, before a
pseudorandom sequence repeats,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.320
is called the period.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.350
The period is strictly
limited by the length

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.360
of the initial seed.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.290
For example, if we
use a two digit seed,

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:31.030
then an algorithm can
produce at most 100 numbers

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.100
before reusing a seed
and repeating the cycle.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.970
A three digit seed can
expand past 1,000 numbers

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.910
before repeating its cycle.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:45.060
And a four digit seed can
expand past 10,000 numbers

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.330
before repeating.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.600
Though if we use a
seed large enough,

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.930
the sequence can expand
into trillions and trillions

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:58.130
of digits before repeating,
though the key difference

00:03:58.130 --> 00:03:59.760
is important.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.210
When you generate
numbers pseudorandomly,

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:06.500
there are many sequences,
which cannot occur.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.990
For example, Alice generates
a truly random sequence

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:11.710
of 20 shifts.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.210
It's equivalent to
a uniform selection

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:17.890
from the stack of all
possible sequences of shifts.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:20.579
This stack contains
26 to the power

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:24.540
20 pages, which is
astronomical in size.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.370
If we stood at the bottom
and shined a light upwards,

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:29.540
a person at the
top would not see

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:33.130
the light for around
200 million years.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:37.040
Compare this to Alice generating
a 20 digit pseudorandom

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:41.140
sequence using a four
digit random seed.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.230
Now this is equivalent
to a uniform selection

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:48.010
from 10,000 possible
initial seeds.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:52.850
Meaning, she can only generate
10,000 different sequences,

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.170
which is a vanishingly
small fraction

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.390
of all possible sequences.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.800
When we move from random
to pseudorandom shifts,

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.400
we shrink the key space into a
much, much smaller seed space.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.940
So for a pseudorandom
sequence to be

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.950
indistinguishable from a
randomly generated sequence,

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:17.380
it must be impractical for
a computer to try all seeds

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:19.130
and look for a match.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.620
This leads to an important
distinction in computer science

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.730
between what is
possible, versus what

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:29.170
is possible in a
reasonable amount of time.

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:32.070
We use the same logic
when we buy a bike lock.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:35.980
We know that anybody can simply
try all possible combinations

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.370
until they find a
match and it opens.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.210
But it would take
them days to do so.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:42.520
So for eight hours.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:46.550
We assume it's practically safe.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:49.520
With pseudorandom
generators, the security

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.120
increases as the length
of the seed increases.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.940
If the most powerful computer
would take hundreds of years

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.470
to run through all
seeds, then we safely

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:05.620
can assume it's practically
secure, instead of perfectly

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:08.180
secure.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:11.040
As computers get
faster, the seed size

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.340
must increase accordingly.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.060
Pseudorandomness
frees Alice and Bob

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:19.890
from having to share
their entire random shift

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.020
sequence in advance.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:25.860
Instead, they share a
relatively short random seed,

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:29.100
and expand it into the same
random looking sequence

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.800
when needed.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:33.590
But what happens
if they can never

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.800
meet to share this random seed?

